<!--
  User-profile-level timeout override for the CH pilot. Mounted at
  /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/timeouts.xml.

  CH 26.3 records per-query `with_setting("http_receive_timeout", ...)`
  in `system.query_log.Settings` but does NOT propagate it to the
  Poco-level socket read on the HTTP body chunked stream. Only this
  profile-level XML override takes effect for the wire behaviour.
  Verified empirically on the 10 k-ledger smoke at 62016000 (task
  0206 follow-up) — failed identically with and without the per-query
  override, fixed only after this XML landed.

  Timeouts at 2 hours — 30 min was insufficient for 64k-ledger
  partitions which take ~80 min wall-clock. Sparse tables
  (lp_positions ~1700 rows / 84 KiB total per partition;
  wasm_interface_metadata ~100 rows / 5 KiB total) never fill the
  client's 256 KiB chunk buffer, so chunks send only at partition
  end. 30 min cap fired on these idle streams. 2h cap covers any
  realistic single-partition workload, including future K=4 parallel
  scenarios where contention slows individual partitions.

  Server-level `keep_alive_timeout` lives in the sibling
  `config.d/timeouts.xml`. See ADR 0044 history for the full rationale.

  Pilot-only — production deployment needs a narrower posture +
  monitoring.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <http_receive_timeout>7200</http_receive_timeout>
            <http_send_timeout>7200</http_send_timeout>
            <receive_timeout>7200</receive_timeout>
            <send_timeout>7200</send_timeout>

            <!-- Per-query memory cap. With the server-level
                 `max_server_memory_usage = 12 GB` (in config.d), this
                 limits any single INSERT / merge to ~6 GB so two
                 concurrent operations can't combine to exceed the
                 server cap. -->
            <max_memory_usage>6000000000</max_memory_usage>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
